---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WatermarkPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/WatermarkPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="watermark-pdf">
  <WatermarkPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to watermark a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above.</li>
        <li>
          Choose <strong>Text</strong> and type the wording, such as DRAFT, CONFIDENTIAL, a client name or a
          date, or switch to <strong>Image</strong> and drop in a PNG or JPEG logo.
        </li>
        <li>
          Set opacity, rotation, size, colour and position. Turn on tiling to repeat the mark across
          the whole page.
        </li>
        <li>Check the live preview, choose which pages to mark, then press <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The preview is a real render of the page, with the watermark drawn on top with the same geometry as
        the export, so the saved file has the position, angle and coverage you see.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Recommended PDF watermark settings</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[620px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Look</th>
              <th scope="col">Text</th>
              <th scope="col">Size / angle</th>
              <th scope="col">Opacity</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Classic diagonal draft</td>
              <td class="font-mono">DRAFT</td>
              <td>60-80 pt, 45°, centred</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">15-25%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Discreet corner stamp</td>
              <td class="font-mono">Internal use only</td>
              <td>9-11 pt, 0°, bottom left</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">55-70%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Anti-copy tiling</td>
              <td class="font-mono">name@example.com</td>
              <td>14-18 pt, 30°, tiled, 40 pt gap</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">8-12%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Logo on every page</td>
              <td>PNG with transparency</td>
              <td>20-40% scale, 0°, top right</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">60-100%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Light grey at 15% usually keeps the text below more readable than red at 15%, because coloured
        watermarks compete with body text. If the document will be printed in black and white, check that the
        mark is still visible at your chosen opacity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Over the content or behind it?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Over</strong> adds the watermark to the end of the page's content stream, so it is always
        visible. This is the standard behaviour and the right choice for a DRAFT stamp.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Behind</strong> must rebuild the file: each original page is embedded as a single object, the
        watermark is drawn first and the page is drawn on top. Text stays sharp, but the rebuild flattens
        interactive items: links, form fields and comments become part of the page content. On scanned pages
        it has no visible effect, because the opaque white background of the scan hides anything below it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a watermark can and cannot do</h2>
      <p>
        A watermark discourages casual reuse and shows the status of a document at a glance. It is not a
        security control: it is ordinary page content, and anyone with a full PDF editor can try to remove it.
        To trace copies per recipient, tile a low-opacity recipient name across the page. That is much harder
        to remove than a single central stamp, because each tile must be removed without damage to the text
        below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Text watermarks use the fonts built into every PDF reader, which cover the WinAnsi character set:
        Latin letters, digits and common punctuation. Chinese, Japanese, Arabic, Cyrillic and emoji need an
        embedded font file. Make those into a transparent PNG and use image mode.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
